%[text] # Observations
%[text] - Solves the problem with no loop by transposing `t` into a column vector, letting MATLAB broadcast across both dimensions simultaneously to produce a 4×100 matrix
%[text] - Single call to `plot(x, velocity)` draws all four curves at once — MATLAB plots each row automatically
%[text] - Demonstrates that "array operations" in MATLAB can extend to 2D, not just 1D vectors
%[text] - Well-documented: the live script text explains *why* the transpose is needed and acknowledges a loop would also work
%[text] - Trade-off worth discussing: elegant and fast, but requires understanding of matrix dimensions — the loop version is easier to read and debug when you're learning \
%%
%[text] # LCS Nozzle Flow Problem
%[text] Author:\*\*\*\*
%[text] MATLAB Version: R2025b
%[text] Date: 7 Apr 26
%[text] This program calculates and plots the velocity and acceleration of fluid through an LCS nozzle based on the distance, x, along the nozzle from 0-4m and time, t, as the throttle is increased from 50% at t=0 to full ahead at t=30s. The equations are given as: $v(x,t)=\\frac{(10+0.2t)\\pi}{\\frac{\\pi}4(4-0.25x)^2}$
%[text] $a(x,t)=\\frac{0.5}{(4-0.25x)^2}+\\frac{0.5(24+0.5t)^2}{(2-0.125x)^5}$
%[text] We will begin by clearing the workspace, command window, and any figures to give us a clean slate.
clear;
clf; %[output:450f90c0]
clc;
%[text] Then we want to declare any variables we will be needing.
% Define the distance and time variables
x = linspace(0, 4, 100); % Distance from 0 to 4 meters in high fidelity to plot
t = linspace(0, 30, 4); % Time from 0 to 30 seconds
%[text] We want to iterate through plotting the velocity as a function of x and t as 4 separate lines (t=0, 10s, 20s, and 30s) on the same set of axes. This can be done with a nested set of for loops or we can utilize the matrix arithmetic capabilities of MATLAB to significantly simplify the code (as is below). A special note: either the 'x' vector or the 't' vector must be transposed to support the matrix arithmetic as matrix multiplication will not work on matricies with incompatible dimensions.
velocity = ((10 + 0.2 * t') * pi) ./ ((pi / 4) * (4 - 0.25 * x) .^ 2);
accel = (0.5 ./ (4 - 0.25 * x) .^ 2) + ((0.5 * (24 + 0.5 * t') .^ 2) ./ (2 - 0.125 * x) .^ 5);
%[text] We now want to plot these on separate figures and provide appropriate labels for the data to be legible. Separate figures are the easiest way to do this and plotting the velocity and acceleration against 'x' for the various desired times yields the 4 lines on each plot. A higher level of fidelity could be achieved by creating a 3-D plot across all 'x' and all 't' values for the rate of throttle change. This is very similar to the plots used for fuel mapping for an engine tuning curve.
figure(1);
plot(x, velocity); %[output:450f90c0]
hold on; %[output:450f90c0]
xlabel('Distance (m)'); %[output:450f90c0]
ylabel('Velocity (m/s)'); %[output:450f90c0]
title('Velocity of Fluid through LCS Nozzle'); %[output:450f90c0]
legend('t=0s', 't=10s', 't=20s', 't=30s'); %[output:450f90c0]
hold off; %[output:450f90c0]

figure(2); %[output:520e8f08]
plot(x, accel); %[output:520e8f08]
hold on; %[output:520e8f08]
xlabel('Distance (m)'); %[output:520e8f08]
ylabel('Acceleration (m/s^2)'); %[output:520e8f08]
title('Acceleration of Fluid through LCS Nozzle'); %[output:520e8f08]
legend('t=0s', 't=10s', 't=20s', 't=30s'); %[output:520e8f08]
hold off; %[output:520e8f08]

